        <h2>Summary / Description</h2>
        <div class="description">
          <div><b>Objective:</b></div><div>Physiological adaptations to long-duration microgravity environments and deficits in perceptual and motor 
function during gravitational transitions are likely to result in reduced functional capacity after landing either on 
Earth or at an exploration destination, such as water-based Orion landings or Mars surface landings. All astronauts 
experience significant decrements in functional performance upon return from a microgravity environment. The 
losses in functional performance can largely be attributed to neurovestibular/sensorimotor deficits that can take 
days or weeks to recover from. Currently, there are no operational countermeasures, but crewmembers need to be 
capable of egressing their landing vehicle unassisted. This study aims to accelerate the operational planning and 
implementation of how to execute this testing in the dynamic, operational environments of Soyuz, Boeing, and 
SpaceX landing zones. Similar testing is planned for the CIPHER study on various missions.
The primary aim of this study is to allow the feasibility of the R+0/1 Egress Fitness CIPHER study tasks to be 
established before collecting data on the first CIPHER crew member. The investigators hypothesize that 
reductions in unassisted capsule egress and planetary EVA task performance will be observed after all mission 
durations. They also hypothesize that individual variability in planetary EVA task performance will depend on the 
task type and will be related to pre-flight fitness parameters, maintenance of in-flight muscle function, 
cardiorespiratory fitness, and sensorimotor function.
The overall objectives of the Egress Fitness study are twofold. The investigators seek to:
1.) Quantify post-landing time course changes in unassisted capsule egress and planetary EVA task 
performance, and
2.) Characterize the recovery to acceptable planetary EVA task performance as a function of EVA task type, 
time in space, time post-landing, and other potential determinants of performance (e.g., sleep, nutrition, 
exercise, sensorimotor performance).<b>
</b></div><div><b><br></b></div><div><b>Approach:</b></div><div>Data will be collected for spaceflight missions ranging from two months, six months, and up to one year in 
duration. Six subjects, ideally two crew from each landing vehicle, will undergo three pre-flight sessions: EVA 
Suit Fit Check, Partial Planetary EVA & Familiarization activities (EVAs), and Unassisted Mock-up Capsule 
Egress & Familiarization to quantify performance parameters in an operationally relevant environment. Postflight 
sessions include the Unassisted Mock-up Capsule Egress on R+0 and Partial Planetary EVA on R+1.
Prior to the Partial Planetary EVA Circuit, crew members will be measured for proper fit and size in a MKIII or 
xEMU EVA suit. After the suit check there is no preferred order for the remaining sessions, unless they are 
scheduled on the same day. In such case, the Unassisted Mock-up Capsule Egress and Familiarization should be 
scheduled first. The Partial Planetary EVA Circuit consists of actions, movements, and component tasks 
sequenced to provide realistic post-landing EVA scenarios. The tasks are sequenced to allow individual 
evaluation on the performance of each specific sub-element on the overall EVA timeline. Tasks include 
translation tasks necessary for crew members to move from a lander to a habitat upon landing on Mars, as well as 
tasks that may be particularly influenced by spaceflight deconditioning. Prior to the start of the egress tasks, the 
crew member will don a heart rate monitor and lay in a recumbent position on a yoga mat. During the timed 
egress, the crew member will attain an upright position, deploy and secure the top hatch ladder, lift a 
backpackstyle survival pack out of the capsule, walk up the ladder (simulating a top hatch egress) until the hip 
clears the hatch, then come back down. The crew member will then walk to a safe waypoint and doff the Heart 
Rate Monitor. When crew return in a Boeing or SpaceX vehicle, the Unassisted Mock-up Capsule Egress will be 
performed on R+0 at the rally airport. When crew return on a Soyuz vehicle, a partial Unassisted Mock-up
Capsule Egress will be at the rally airport on R+0, and the full Unassisted Mock-up Capsule Egress will be 
conducted at the refueling airport on R+0. The Partial Planetary EVA Circuit will be on R+1 at JSC, as this 
requires the use of the Active Response Gravity Offload System (ARGOS), a permanent facility at JSC. The 
EVA tasks and gravity environment will be the same for both pre- and post-flight sessions.</div><div><b><br></b></div><div><b>Results:</b></div><div>It is critical to understand what crews are capable of, and at which time points they will be able to perform which 
tasks. The research product will be a temporal profile of unassisted capsule egress and planetary EVA task 
performance pre-flight and at multiple post-landing intervals, the timing of which will be determined based on 
post-landing logistics and coordination with other investigations. By having participants perform unassisted 
capsule egress task and a partial planetary EVA soon after landing, the data from this study will verify methods of
execution of these egress tests in the operational environment of Soyuz, and Boeing and SpaceX landings.</div>
        </div>

